//? Title: The Bailey–Borwein–Plouffe Formula
//? Subtitle: Proving the series, extracting deep hexadecimal digits of π without the earlier ones, measuring how far double precision reaches, and testing the digits for randomness, with datamunge's algorithms::bbp_pi_hex_digits and an exact Machin reference
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Bailey–Borwein–Plouffe series \(\pi=\sum_k16^{-k}\bigl(\frac4{8k+1}-\frac2{8k+4}-\frac1{8k+5}-\frac1{8k+6}\bigr)\)
lets one compute hexadecimal digits of \(\pi\) at position \(n\) without any of the earlier
digits, using modular exponentiation and constant memory. We prove the series from an integral
of a rational function and derive the digit-extraction scheme. We then check it against the
exact digits, which `datamunge` now computes from Machin's formula in fixed-point arithmetic and
which agree with GNU MPFR for the first 100,000 digits. In double precision BBP was right at all
20,000 positions tested, both one digit and eight digits at a time. Reading ten digits per
evaluation failed in 55 of 2,000 evaluations. The error of \(\{16^n\pi\}\) grows roughly like
\(\sqrt n\,2^{-53}\), as for a random walk of rounding errors. At positions \(10^6\) and \(10^7\)
one evaluation still gives 10 and 8 correct digits of the published values. The cost grows
linearly in \(n\) (slope 1.06 on a log–log plot), reaching about 2 seconds at \(n=10^7\) on this
machine. The first 50,000 hex digits pass \(\chi^2\) tests of uniform single digits
(\(p=0.86\)) and digit pairs (\(p=0.66\)). This agrees with, but cannot prove, the conjecture
that \(\pi\) is normal in base 16.
)

\toc

> A series for π in base 16

In 1995 Bailey, Borwein and Plouffe found, by computer search, the series

$$
\pi=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac1{16^k}\Bigl(\frac4{8k+1}-\frac2{8k+4}-\frac1{8k+5}-\frac1{8k+6}\Bigr)
=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac1{16^k}\,\frac{120k^2+151k+47}{512k^4+1024k^3+712k^2+194k+15}.
$$
\alttext(pi equals the sum over k from 0 to infinity of 1 over 16 to the k, times 4 over 8k plus 1, minus 2 over 8k plus 4, minus 1 over 8k plus 5, minus 1 over 8k plus 6. Combined over a common denominator, the bracket is 120 k squared plus 151 k plus 47, over 512 k to the fourth plus 1024 k cubed plus 712 k squared plus 194 k plus 15.)

Each term adds a little more than one hexadecimal digit, which is slow by modern standards.
What makes the formula remarkable is that the powers of 16 let a single hexadecimal digit far
out in the expansion be computed without computing any digit before it.

>> Proof

For \(1\le j\le8\), expand \(1/(1-x^8)\) as a geometric series and integrate term by term:

$$
\int_0^{1/\sqrt2}\frac{x^{j-1}}{1-x^8}\,dx=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\int_0^{1/\sqrt2}x^{j-1+8k}\,dx
=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{(1/\sqrt2)^{8k+j}}{8k+j}=\frac1{\sqrt2^{\,j}}\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac1{16^k(8k+j)}.
$$
\alttext(The integral from 0 to 1 over root 2 of x to the j minus 1 over 1 minus x to the 8 equals the sum over k of the integral of x to the j minus 1 plus 8k, which equals the sum of 1 over root 2 to the 8k plus j, over 8k plus j, which equals 1 over root 2 to the j times the sum over k of 1 over 16 to the k times 8k plus j.)

So each of the four sums in the BBP series is an integral. Multiplying by \(\sqrt2^{\,j}\) and
combining with the weights \(4,-2,-1,-1\) for \(j=1,4,5,6\) gives

$$
\sum_k\frac1{16^k}\Bigl(\frac4{8k+1}-\frac2{8k+4}-\frac1{8k+5}-\frac1{8k+6}\Bigr)
=\int_0^{1/\sqrt2}\frac{4\sqrt2-8x^3-4\sqrt2\,x^4-8x^5}{1-x^8}\,dx
=\int_0^1\frac{16y-16}{y^4-2y^3+4y-4}\,dy,
$$
\alttext(The BBP sum equals the integral from 0 to 1 over root 2 of 4 root 2 minus 8 x cubed minus 4 root 2 x to the fourth minus 8 x to the fifth, over 1 minus x to the 8, which after substituting y equals root 2 x becomes the integral from 0 to 1 of 16 y minus 16 over y to the fourth minus 2 y cubed plus 4 y minus 4.)

after the substitution \(y=\sqrt2\,x\). The denominator factors as \((y^2-2)(y^2-2y+2)\), and
partial fractions give

$$
\frac{16y-16}{(y^2-2)(y^2-2y+2)}=\frac{4y}{y^2-2}-\frac{4y-8}{y^2-2y+2},
$$
\alttext(16 y minus 16 over y squared minus 2 times y squared minus 2 y plus 2 equals 4 y over y squared minus 2, minus 4 y minus 8 over y squared minus 2 y plus 2.)

whose antiderivative is \(2\log|y^2-2|-2\log(y^2-2y+2)+4\arctan(y-1)\). Between 0 and 1 this is
\((0-0+0)-(2\log2-2\log2-\pi)=\pi\). \(\blacksquare\)

>> Extracting one digit

The hexadecimal digits of \(\pi\) from position \(n\) on are the expansion of the fractional part
\(\{16^n\pi\}\), where position 0 is the first digit after the point. Write
\(S_j=\sum_k16^{-k}/(8k+j)\), so \(\{16^n\pi\}=\{4\{16^nS_1\}-2\{16^nS_4\}-\{16^nS_5\}-\{16^nS_6\}\}\). Split
each sum at \(k=n\):

$$
\{16^nS_j\}=\Bigl\{\sum_{k=0}^{n}\frac{16^{n-k}\bmod(8k+j)}{8k+j}+\sum_{k=n+1}^\infty\frac{16^{n-k}}{8k+j}\Bigr\}.
$$
\alttext(The fractional part of 16 to the n times S j equals the fractional part of the sum for k from 0 to n of 16 to the n minus k mod 8k plus j, over 8k plus j, plus the sum for k greater than n of 16 to the n minus k over 8k plus j.)

For \(k\le n\) the term \(16^{n-k}/(8k+j)\) is an integer plus \((16^{n-k}\bmod(8k+j))/(8k+j)\), and
the integer cannot affect a fractional part, so it is dropped. The remainder comes from binary
modular exponentiation in \(O(\log n)\) multiplications of numbers below \(8n+j\). The tail with
\(k>n\) shrinks by a factor of 16 per term. So one digit costs \(O(n\log n)\) word operations
and constant memory, with no arithmetic on numbers longer than a machine word. Every
intermediate sum is reduced modulo 1, so it stays in \([0,1)\).

The catch is rounding. Each of the roughly \(4n\) additions rounds to the 53-bit double
format, with an error of up to \(2^{-53}\). If these errors behave like independent random
signs, the accumulated error grows like \(\sqrt n\,2^{-53}\) instead of \(n\,2^{-53}\). A hex
digit at offset \(i\) is right when the error is smaller than the distance from \(16^i\{16^n\pi\}\)
to the nearest integer. About \(\log_{16}(1/\text{error})\) leading digits of each evaluation
can be trusted.

`algorithms::bbp_pi_fraction(n)` returns \(\{16^n\pi\}\), and `algorithms::bbp_pi_hex_digits(n, c)`
reads \(c\le12\) digits from it. To check them we need the true digits. `algorithms::pi_hex_digits(N)`
computes the first \(N\) exactly from Machin's formula
\(\pi=16\arctan\tfrac15-4\arctan\tfrac1{239}\), in fixed-point arithmetic on 32-bit limbs. Its
first 100,000 digits agree exactly with the GNU MPFR library's value of \(\pi\).

> Every digit checked

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bbp_precision.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  constexpr int N = 20000;
  const std::string exact = alg::pi_hex_digits(N);
  std::print("π = 3.");
  for (int g = 0; g < 8; ++g) std::print("{} ", exact.substr(8 * g, 8));
  std::print("…\n\n");

  std::print("| digits read per evaluation | evaluations | digits checked | evaluations that disagree |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const int c : {1, 8, 10}) {
    int evaluations = 0, wrong = 0;
    for (int n = 0; n + c <= N; n += c, ++evaluations)
      if (alg::bbp_pi_hex_digits(n, c) != exact.substr(n, c)) ++wrong;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", c, evaluations, evaluations * c, wrong);
  }
  std::print("\n");

  // Error of {16^n pi} against the exact digits, at roughly log-spaced positions.
  const auto exact_fraction = [](const std::string& hex) {
    double f = 0.0, scale = 1.0 / 16.0;
    for (const char c : hex) {
      f += scale * static_cast<double>(c <= '9' ? c - '0' : c - 'A' + 10);
      scale /= 16.0;
    }
    return f;
  };
  const auto matching = [](const std::string& a, const std::string& b) {
    std::size_t k = 0;
    while (k < a.size() && k < b.size() && a[k] == b[k]) ++k;
    return static_cast<double>(k);
  };
  std::vector<double> log_n, log_err, digits_ok;
  for (double x = 0.0; x <= std::log10(N - 20.0); x += 0.02) {
    const int n = static_cast<int>(std::pow(10.0, x));
    const double err = std::abs(alg::bbp_pi_fraction(n) - exact_fraction(exact.substr(n, 14)));
    log_n.push_back(std::log10(n + 1.0));
    log_err.push_back(std::log10(std::max(err, 1e-18)));
    digits_ok.push_back(matching(alg::bbp_pi_hex_digits(n, 12), exact.substr(n, 12)));
  }
  // Published digits at 1-based positions 10^6 and 10^7 (Bailey, Borwein and Plouffe 1997).
  std::print("| zero-based position n | BBP, 12 digits | published digits | correct leading digits | error of \\(\\{{16^n\\pi\\}}\\) |\n"
             "| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [n, reference] : {std::pair{999999, std::string("26C65E52CB4593")}, {9999999, std::string("17AF5863EFED8D")}}) {
    const double err = std::abs(alg::bbp_pi_fraction(n) - exact_fraction(reference));
    const std::string bbp = alg::bbp_pi_hex_digits(n, 12);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.1e} |\n", n, bbp, reference, matching(bbp, reference), err);
    log_n.push_back(std::log10(n + 1.0));
    log_err.push_back(std::log10(err));
    digits_ok.push_back(matching(bbp, reference));
  }

  std::vector<double> line_x{0.0, 7.2}, line_y;
  for (const double x : line_x) line_y.push_back(std::log10(std::pow(2.0, -53.0)) + 0.5 * x);
  auto err_plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                      .lines(line_x, line_y, "√n · 2^-53", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5)
                      .points(log_n, log_err, "BBP error", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0)
                      .x_limits(0, 7.2)
                      .y_limits(-18.2, -10)
                      .title("Error of the BBP fraction")
                      .x_label("log10(n + 1)")
                      .y_label("log10 |error|");
  auto digit_plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                        .points(log_n, digits_ok, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0)
                        .x_limits(0, 7.2)
                        .y_limits(0, 13)
                        .title("Correct leading hex digits per evaluation")
                        .x_label("log10(n + 1)")
                        .y_label("digits (of 12)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(err_plot).add(digit_plot).size(1200, 440).save_svg("build/bbp_precision.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
π = 3.243F6A88 85A308D3 13198A2E 03707344 A4093822 299F31D0 082EFA98 EC4E6C89 …

| digits read per evaluation | evaluations | digits checked | evaluations that disagree |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 20000 | 20000 | 0 |
| 8 | 2500 | 20000 | 0 |
| 10 | 2000 | 20000 | 55 |

| zero-based position n | BBP, 12 digits | published digits | correct leading digits | error of \(\{16^n\pi\}\) |
| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| 999999 | 26C65E52CB31 | 26C65E52CB4593 | 10 | 7.1e-14 |
| 9999999 | 17AF5863F0F5 | 17AF5863EFED8D | 8 | 9.4e-13 |
\image(figures/bbp_precision.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: absolute error of \(\{16^n\pi\}\) computed by BBP in double precision, against position, with the line \(\sqrt n\,2^{-53}\); errors below \(10^{-18}\) are drawn at \(-18\). Right: how many of the 12 digits read from one evaluation are correct. The two rightmost points use the published digits at positions \(10^6\) and \(10^7\).)
\alttext(Left: scatter of log10 error against log10(n + 1), from 0 to 4.3 plus two points at 6 and 7. Errors rise from about 10 to the -16 at small n to about 10 to the -13.5 at n = 20,000, -13.1 at 10^6 and -12.0 at 10^7, scattered around a grey line of slope one half; one point sits at the floor of -18. Right: number of correct leading digits out of 12: 12 for small n, mostly 11 from n of about 10 on, with more 10s beyond n of 1,000, two 9s, then 10 at 10^6 and 8 at 10^7.)

All 20,000 single digits and all 2,500 eight-digit blocks are correct. Ten digits per evaluation is
too many: 55 of the 2,000 blocks have a wrong tenth digit. The left panel shows why. The error of the
computed fraction rises with \(n\) at about the slope \(\tfrac12\) of the line
\(\sqrt n\,2^{-53}\), the random-walk model of §1.2. Near \(n=2\times10^4\) it reaches about
\(10^{-13.5}\), close to the size \(16^{-10}\approx10^{-12}\) of a tenth digit. A digit fails when
the true fraction lies within the error of a digit boundary, so failures start as occasional
and become common as the error approaches the digit's size.

The published deep digits confirm the trend. At \(n=999{,}999\), the first 10 of the 12 digits
are right (error \(7.1\times10^{-14}\)). At \(n=9{,}999{,}999\) the first 8 are right (error
\(9.4\times10^{-13}\), about 2.7 times the line). Record computations use BBP-type formulas
for exactly this kind of check: they compute a few digits near the end of a full expansion and
compare. They therefore read only the first few digits of each evaluation, or use higher
precision.

> Cost

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bbp_cost.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| position n | 8 digits from n | 8 digits from n + 1 | digits that agree | time (ms) |\n| ---: | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> log_n, log_ms;
  for (int e = 4; e <= 14; ++e) {
    const int n = static_cast<int>(std::pow(10.0, e / 2.0));
    const auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    const std::string here = alg::bbp_pi_hex_digits(n, 9);
    const double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - start).count();
    const std::string next = alg::bbp_pi_hex_digits(n + 1, 8);
    int agree = 0;
    while (agree < 8 && here[agree + 1] == next[agree]) ++agree;
    log_n.push_back(std::log10(n));
    log_ms.push_back(std::log10(ms));
    if (e % 2 == 0) std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.3g} |\n", n, here.substr(0, 8), next, agree, ms);
  }
  const double slope = (log_ms.back() - log_ms[log_ms.size() - 5]) / (log_n.back() - log_n[log_n.size() - 5]);
  std::print("\nSlope of log time against log n over the last two decades: {:.2f}.\n", slope);

  std::vector<double> ref_x{log_n.front(), log_n.back()};
  std::vector<double> ref_y{log_ms.back() - (log_n.back() - log_n.front()), log_ms.back()};
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(ref_x, ref_y, "slope 1", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5)
      .lines(log_n, log_ms, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.5)
      .points(log_n, log_ms, "measured", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 7.0)
      .y_limits(-2.3, 4.3)
      .title("Time to extract digits at position n")
      .x_label("log10 n")
      .y_label("log10 time (ms)")
      .size(800, 440)
      .save_svg("build/bbp_cost.svg");
}
```
